<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deism is the belief that a Supreme Being created the universe and that reason and observation of the natural world alone are sufficient to establish the existence of this creator, without prophecy, revelation or religious texts. Many deists hold that God does not intervene in the world after creating it, and the position stresses natural theology, in which God's existence is revealed through nature. Deists set out this critique of organised religion from the 17th century, especially in 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Deism saw a revival in early-19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and North America, and several of its tenets continued in movements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unitarianism">Unitarianism</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars trace rationalistic theologies to ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophanes">Xenophanes</a> of the 6th century BCE has been described as having a one-God theology, and the Stoic Chrysippus of the 3rd century BCE affirmed that the universe itself is God. The French word déiste first appeared in 1563 in a treatise by the Swiss Calvinist theologian Pierre Viret, and the English word in its present meaning appears in Robert Burton's Anatomy of Melancholy of 1621. Deism developed into a movement only after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a>. Lord Herbert of Cherbury's De Veritate of 1624 was the first major statement of it in English; Herbert held that five Common Notions, among them one Supreme God and the duty to worship him, unify all religious beliefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>'s Essay Concerning Human Understanding of 1690 attacked innate ideas, so later deists turned to arguments from experience and nature, and under the influence of Newton to the argument from design. John Toland's Christianity Not Mysterious of 1696 marks the start of this phase, in which Matthew Tindal, Anthony Collins, Conyers Middleton and others wrote.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> deism made two assertions: that reason and the natural world are a valid source of religious knowledge, and that revelation is not. Its writers held that God exists and created the universe and gave humans the ability to reason. Its critical side rejected books claiming divine revelation, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, the doctrine of the Trinity, and reports of miracles and prophecies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In North America English deism influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, who called himself a Unitarian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a> defended deism in The Age of Reason (1793–1794). In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the best-known deist was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, who met English deism and Newtonian science during his exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1726 to 1728. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> the deistic Cult of the Supreme Being, an expression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a>'s views, was briefly the state religion, for just under three months.</p><p>Peter Gay dates the slow decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> deism from the 1730s. Suggested causes include naturalism and materialism, the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> on the limits of reason, the violence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and Christian revivals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodism</a>. Biblical criticism, one of the deists' activities, became a discipline of its own, and deist rejection of revealed religion contributed to 19th-century liberal British theology and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unitarianism">Unitarianism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Church membership was low because it had not kept up with population growth, and Enlightenment rationalism was leading many people towards atheism, <b>Deism</b>, Unitarianism and Universalism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Some on the right only wanted a rationalist approach; on the extreme left some ventured towards <b>Deism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/haskalah">Haskalah · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
